| Tool / artifact | Main use demonstrated |
| :---- | :---- |
| Hardware write blocker | Protect original evidence from writes |
| Registry `StorageDevicePolicies\WriteProtect` | Fallback USB write-protection setting |
| dd for Windows | Create raw physical disk images |
| Sleuth Kit `fsstat` | Identify and inspect image file systems |
| OSFMount | Mount images read-only for inspection |
| Windows Defender | Scan mounted evidence for potential malware |
| Sysinternals Autoruns | Inspect startup/persistence entries, including offline Windows installations |
| Autopsy | Create cases, ingest evidence, analyze artifacts, extract files, and report |
| Windows Registry Recovery | Browse parsed registry hives |
| RegRipper | Extract selected registry artifacts into reports |
| `TypedURLs` | Internet Explorer address-bar URL artifacts |
| `USBSTOR` | USB storage device history in registry artifacts |
| `docProps/core.xml` | Office document creator, timestamps, and revisions |
| EXIF reader | Camera, timestamp, and optional GPS photo metadata |
| ChromeCacheView | Analyze Chrome cache artifacts |
| ChromeHistoryView | Analyze Chrome browsing-history database |
| MozillaCacheView | Analyze Firefox cache artifacts |
| Raw email headers | Examine sender/recipient claims, routing, timestamps, and server information |

## Likely exam-study priorities

The instructor specifically says that some of the demonstrated **tools and functions** may be tested. Prioritize knowing **what each tool does**, **which evidence artifact it examines**, **what information it can reveal**, and **why evidence integrity and careful documentation matter**. The lecture's practice exercises are described as ungraded but recommended.